WEBVTT

00:00.470 --> 00:01.750
- [Instructor] We are
told we want to solve

00:01.750 --> 00:03.560
the following equation,

00:03.560 --> 00:05.674
the negative natural log of two x

00:05.674 --> 00:08.070
is equal to two times the absolute value

00:08.070 --> 00:11.440
of x minus four, all of that minus seven.

00:11.440 --> 00:14.930
One of the solutions is x is equal to 0.5.

00:14.930 --> 00:16.450
Find the other solution.

00:16.450 --> 00:18.310
They say hint, use a graphing calculator

00:18.310 --> 00:21.170
and round your answer
to the nearest tenth.

00:21.170 --> 00:24.730
So pause this video and have
a go at this if you like,

00:24.730 --> 00:26.070
and then we'll work on this together.

00:26.070 --> 00:27.795
And I encourage you to have a go on it,

00:27.795 --> 00:29.500
(laughing) a go at it.

00:29.500 --> 00:31.570
All right now let's work on this together.

00:31.570 --> 00:33.530
Now the key here is to
realize that we might be able

00:33.530 --> 00:34.560
to solve this by graphing,

00:34.560 --> 00:38.130
or at least approximate the
solutions to this by graphing.

00:38.130 --> 00:39.680
And the way we do that is,

00:39.680 --> 00:40.800
if we have an equation,

00:40.800 --> 00:43.195
especially a hairy equation
like this, in one variable,

00:43.195 --> 00:45.810
we can set y equal to the left

00:45.810 --> 00:47.300
and then set y equal to the right

00:47.300 --> 00:49.680
and then graph each of those functions

00:49.680 --> 00:51.940
and then think about where they intersect.

00:51.940 --> 00:54.210
Because they'll intersect at an x-value

00:54.210 --> 00:56.050
that gives us the same y-value,

00:56.050 --> 00:58.410
and that means that the
two sides are the same.

00:58.410 --> 01:00.030
So what do I mean by that?

01:00.030 --> 01:01.720
Well we could set y is equal

01:01.720 --> 01:04.000
to the negative natural log of two x.

01:04.000 --> 01:08.300
So we could have one equation
or one function like that.

01:08.300 --> 01:12.000
And then we could have
another equation or function

01:12.000 --> 01:14.710
that y is equal to two times

01:14.710 --> 01:18.520
the absolute value of x
minus four minus seven.

01:18.520 --> 01:19.930
And let's see where they intersect.

01:19.930 --> 01:21.640
And the x-values where they intersect,

01:21.640 --> 01:23.770
those are going to be solutions to that.

01:23.770 --> 01:27.910
So I'm going to use Desmos
as my graphing calculator.

01:27.910 --> 01:30.010
So let's type in the two sides.

01:30.010 --> 01:31.670
So first I'll do the left side.

01:31.670 --> 01:36.250
So if y is equal to the negative

01:36.250 --> 01:40.340
natural log of two x.

01:40.340 --> 01:43.880
And actually, let me make
my color to be the same

01:43.880 --> 01:47.740
or as close as I can, so maybe
closer to that bluer color.

01:47.740 --> 01:52.740
Okay and then the net
one, I want y is equal

01:52.810 --> 01:56.520
to two times,

01:56.520 --> 01:59.830
two times the absolute value,

01:59.830 --> 02:02.610
actually I don't know
whether Desmos prefers,

02:02.610 --> 02:04.460
I'll use that, actually that works, okay,

02:04.460 --> 02:09.430
x minus four, and then I
will close my absolute value,

02:09.430 --> 02:12.130
and then I have minus seven.

02:12.130 --> 02:14.720
And I will do this in the red color

02:14.720 --> 02:16.853
so that we can keep track of things.

02:18.220 --> 02:19.800
Okay.

02:19.800 --> 02:22.200
So those are my two graphs.

02:22.200 --> 02:24.890
And now, I just need to think
about where they intersect.

02:24.890 --> 02:27.300
And one of the solutions
is x equal to 0.5.

02:27.300 --> 02:28.580
That's not the one they want.

02:28.580 --> 02:31.510
They want the other solution so to speak.

02:31.510 --> 02:33.313
So let's see.

02:34.280 --> 02:36.240
So let's see, we have one solution,

02:36.240 --> 02:38.670
actually let me zoom in a little bit.

02:38.670 --> 02:42.570
So when x is equal to
0.5, that's this solution,

02:42.570 --> 02:44.650
that's this solution right over here.

02:44.650 --> 02:48.610
It looks like y is equal to zero there.

02:48.610 --> 02:50.990
But then the other point of intersection

02:50.990 --> 02:52.920
seems to be right over here,

02:52.920 --> 02:54.880
and actually Desmos has
a nice little feature

02:54.880 --> 02:57.920
where it'll tell us that
point right over there.

02:57.920 --> 02:59.300
But you could even approximate it.

02:59.300 --> 03:01.860
You can see that x is over six

03:01.860 --> 03:04.360
and that each of these,
let's see one, two, three,

03:04.360 --> 03:07.330
four, five, each of those is .2.

03:07.330 --> 03:10.550
So it's going to be 6.2 something

03:10.550 --> 03:12.286
is what I would do, and
they want us to round

03:12.286 --> 03:14.230
to the nearest tenth anyway.

03:14.230 --> 03:15.670
So you don't even need
to use that feature,

03:15.670 --> 03:18.010
but you can see very
clearly that when x is equal

03:18.010 --> 03:21.250
to approximately 6.238,

03:21.250 --> 03:25.470
that we get a y is equal to negative 2.54.

03:25.470 --> 03:28.140
Or another way to think about it is,

03:28.140 --> 03:32.100
when x is approximately equal to,

03:32.100 --> 03:35.790
when x is approximately equal to 6.2,

03:36.772 --> 03:38.635
that the two sides of this equation

03:38.635 --> 03:40.990
are going to be equal to each other,

03:40.990 --> 03:43.320
are approximately equal to each other.

03:43.320 --> 03:45.999
And we're done, we've just solved using a,

03:45.999 --> 03:49.030
or at least approximated
a solution using graphing

03:49.030 --> 03:50.280
in a graphing calculator.
